
/*! \page about_programs_page Programs

\ref about_history_page  (Prev)   &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref about_features_page (Next)
<BR>

The PSCF package currently contains source code for two programs 
named pscf_rp and pscf_r1d. The purpose and capabilties of each
are summarized below;

<b>pscf_rp</b>:

The pscf_rp program can perform either SCFT or PS-FTS calculations 
for systems with periodic boundary conditions in 1, 2 or 3 dimensions.
This program provides capabilities for SCFT analogous to those of the 
older Fortran PSCF program, and also provides a complete set of tools
for PS-FTS simulations.  The suffix "rp" stands for "real periodic".

The pscf_rp program can be compiled to create either an executable that
uses only CPU hardware or a GPU-enabled executable that uses an NVIDIA
GPU. The CPU-only version is compiled to create an executable file named 
pscf_rpc, while the GPU-enabled version is compiled to create an executable 
named pscf_rpg. These two executable files provide identical capabilities, 
use identical formats for all input and output files, and produce 
identical results to within small differences in round-off errors. 
The pscf_rpc and pscf_rpg executables are described throughout this 
manual as different versions of the same program, which we refer to by 
the generic program name pscf_rp. 

<b>pscf_r1d</b>:

The pscf_r1d program is a comparatively simple CPU program for 
one-dimensional SCFT calculations. Calculations can performed for 
problems with planar, cylindrical or spherical symmetry, using Cartesian, 
cylindrical or spherical coordinate systems. A finite-difference spatial 
discretization scheme is used to solve the underlying modified diffusion 
equation (MDE). This program is useful for SCFT problems involving flat 
or curved interfaces, and for simulating spherical or cylindrical 
micelles. The pscf_r1d program uses standard CPU hardware, and cannot 
perform stochastic field theoretic simulations.

<BR>
